WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:03.169
Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award-nominated,

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.404
Emmy, Grammy, and Tony
Award-winning actress

00:00:05.404 --> 00:00:08.007
you know from "Gypsy,"
"The First Wives Club,"

00:00:08.007 --> 00:00:10.276
and the Broadway revival
of "Hello Dolly."

00:00:10.276 --> 00:00:12.545
Her annual
Hulaween costume gala,

00:00:12.545 --> 00:00:14.981
"In Search of
the Sanderson Sisters:

00:00:14.981 --> 00:00:17.650
A Hocus Pocus Takeover
of Hulaween,"

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.286
will stream virtually
this Friday at 8:00 p.m.

00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:24.090
Please welcome the legendary
Bette Midler

00:00:24.090 --> 00:00:26.725
How are you, Bette?

00:00:26.725 --> 00:00:28.794
-Uh,deafening applause.
I'm good.

00:00:28.794 --> 00:00:30.696
I'm good. I'm very well.
Thank you.

00:00:30.696 --> 00:00:33.900
-I am so -- I'm genuinely
so thrilled to have you here.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.370
I am a gigantic fan,
and this has --

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.472
This has been a long time
coming for me.

00:00:39.472 --> 00:00:41.474
-I am so thrilled
to hear you say that

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:43.109
because I'm a huge,
huge fan of yours.

00:00:43.109 --> 00:00:44.911
I wouldn't have gotten through
any of this

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:47.046
without you and Stephen Colbert.

00:00:47.046 --> 00:00:49.782
It's just --
You've been fantastic.

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:53.553
This tonight's one -- Tonight's
"Closer Look" was like --

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.821
I mean, I was flipping birds
left and right.

00:00:55.821 --> 00:00:57.690
-Well, thank you so much.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:59.659
You know, you are considered
the queen of many things.

00:00:59.659 --> 00:01:02.495
But for a while now,
you've been considered

00:01:02.495 --> 00:01:07.800
the queen of Halloween because
of the 1993 film "Hocus Pocus."

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.168
Is this true?

00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:11.270
-Yes, yes, it is true.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:13.973
It was a picture that started
off a little bit sleepy,

00:01:13.973 --> 00:01:16.676
but somehow
it found its niche. [nitch]

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:17.910
Niche. [Neesh]

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:19.512
And it became a kind
of a cult classic.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:22.148
And now it's everywhere.
I mean, I wish I had a nickel

00:01:22.148 --> 00:01:24.184
for every person
who turned out to be --

00:01:24.184 --> 00:01:27.487
you know, who turned out in
a Winifred Sanderson costume.

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:30.456
But it's --
Now it's a cult classic.

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:34.460
And after 27 years,
the two girls,

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:36.729
Kathy Najimy
and Sarah Jessica Parker,

00:01:36.729 --> 00:01:40.666
and I have reunited for this
one-night-only Halloween --

00:01:40.666 --> 00:01:44.937
Hulaween extravaganza,
a mockumentary called

00:01:44.937 --> 00:01:47.006
"In Search of
the Sanderson Sisters."

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:48.707
And it's not just a Zoom call.

00:01:48.707 --> 00:01:52.411
And tickets are still available.
NYRP.org.

00:01:52.411 --> 00:01:54.414
-I have a question to ask
because this is interesting.

00:01:54.414 --> 00:01:55.982
A lot of people
are doing Zoom reunions,

00:01:55.982 --> 00:01:58.017
but this is not just talking
about the good old days.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.585
You actually have written
a new script.

00:01:59.585 --> 00:02:01.754
We're going to catch up
with the Sanderson sisters.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.322
I have a question, which is,
are the teeth

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:04.723
gonna show up, Bette?

00:02:04.723 --> 00:02:05.925
-They're definitely
gonna show up.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:07.193
Now, they are not
my original teeth.

00:02:07.193 --> 00:02:08.761
My original teeth did break,

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:11.030
because I have worn them
on special occasions.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.133
But I did get the original
tooth man, Kevin Haney,

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:17.336
who is divine,
to make me another pair.

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:18.872
And they didn't fit.

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:20.807
You know, they wouldn't
stay in my face.

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:22.742
So I found something
called Poligrip.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:24.611
That's what
they told me to put on,

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:27.613
and that stuff really works,
I got to say.

00:02:27.613 --> 00:02:29.449
So I put them in
and, hey, they stayed.

00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:33.052
It was --
It was quite an afternoon.

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.688
And we were --
And we shot with all of us.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:38.725
The two girls showed up.
They came to a studio.

00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:40.093
We did all the COVID stuff.

00:02:40.093 --> 00:02:41.961
And we did our --
You know, we did our thing.

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:45.398
And it was -- And the thing
that was the weirdest part is,

00:02:45.398 --> 00:02:48.534
like, 27 years after
we had finished this picture

00:02:48.534 --> 00:02:51.470
and I haven't seen those two
girls together in 27 years.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:52.805
I've seen them in -- in --

00:02:52.805 --> 00:02:55.942
You know, I've seen them
many times, but always solo.

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:58.110
It was exactly the same.
It was like yesterday.

00:02:58.110 --> 00:02:59.879
It was like we were eating
in the cafeteria.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.481
It was exactly the same.

00:03:01.481 --> 00:03:03.149
Fell right into
our old characters.

00:03:03.149 --> 00:03:05.151
-I imagine when you make a film,
obviously,

00:03:05.151 --> 00:03:06.986
you want it to be a big hit.
That's a goal.

00:03:06.986 --> 00:03:10.056
But is it nice
to have a film like this

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:11.691
that sort of had a longer tail

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:14.694
and that you sort of continually
hear about over the years?

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.430
-I would say so. Oh, yeah.
I would say so.

00:03:17.430 --> 00:03:20.532
It's -- It's actually kind of --

00:03:20.532 --> 00:03:23.436
It's the most --
It's so strange.

00:03:23.436 --> 00:03:25.338
We had so much fun making it.

00:03:25.338 --> 00:03:28.874
And we had no expectation
for it. None at all.

00:03:28.874 --> 00:03:30.342
And as the years have gone by,

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:32.879
it's just become this snowball,
this gigantic snowball.

00:03:32.879 --> 00:03:35.114
And I have to say
it has been so rewarding.

00:03:35.114 --> 00:03:38.718
It's been -- I mean,
people come to me and say,

00:03:38.718 --> 00:03:40.186
"Oh, I saw this when I was 6."

00:03:40.186 --> 00:03:41.854
And these people
have, like, white hair.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:46.659
And you're like, "Oh, my.
I'm sure it was lovely."

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.928
It's great. It's great.

00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:50.562
It's actually
the most rewarding.

00:03:50.562 --> 00:03:53.232
And that's not -- and that's --
I'm talking financially, too.

00:03:53.232 --> 00:03:55.301
It's been great.
I mean, it's like an annuity.

00:03:55.301 --> 00:03:57.135
It just keeps going.
-That's wonderful.

00:03:57.135 --> 00:04:00.939
It's very nice that -- Hulaween
is usually a live event,

00:04:00.939 --> 00:04:04.076
a live fundraiser for the
New York Restoration Project.

00:04:04.076 --> 00:04:06.713
This year, because of the fact
that we're going through

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:08.814
a pandemic,
people can only pay $10

00:04:08.814 --> 00:04:11.751
because, it should be noted,
your donors have stepped up.

00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.352
-Exactly.

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:16.155
The donors are the sponsors,
and we have quite a few of them.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:18.391
And they have been so generous.
Unbelievably generous.

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.992
But we've sold
a lot of tickets, too.

00:04:19.992 --> 00:04:21.493
And the reason
we've sold tickets

00:04:21.493 --> 00:04:23.763
is because we have
this incredible cast.

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:25.998
It's not just Sarah Jessica
and Kathy.

00:04:25.998 --> 00:04:29.435
But we also have Omri Katz,
Thora Birch,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.004
Doug Jones and Vinessa Shaw
from the original picture.

00:04:32.004 --> 00:04:33.906
And we also have Adam Lambert.

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:37.209
We have Meryl Streep,
Glenn Close, Sarah Silverman.

00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:40.280
We have Marty Short.
We have Jennifer Hudson.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.115
We have Anjelah Johnson,
Kenan Thompson.

00:04:43.115 --> 00:04:45.885
We have a huge cast.
Billy Crystal.

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:48.754
Billy Eichner. Alex Brightman.
Alex Moffat.

00:04:48.754 --> 00:04:50.957
And John Stamos
is the handsome devil.

00:04:50.957 --> 00:04:53.359
And it was wrangling --

00:04:53.359 --> 00:04:56.896
a lot of talent wrangling
and a lot of effects

00:04:56.896 --> 00:05:00.733
and a lot of excitement about
building effects and what --

00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:02.835
what can we do here
that will be a surprise?

00:05:02.835 --> 00:05:04.804
So it was -- We --
I think it's --

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:07.306
I feel that we put a lot
of creativity into it

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:10.143
because when people come
to our in-person event,

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:12.345
they expect a real blowout.

00:05:12.345 --> 00:05:14.947
And we wanted
to give them something

00:05:14.947 --> 00:05:16.582
that would be memorable.

00:05:16.582 --> 00:05:20.386
And I got to say.
It's 10 bucks. Don't panic.

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.788
We did a lot of work.
I hope you like it.

00:05:22.788 --> 00:05:24.156
If you don't, what can I say?

00:05:24.156 --> 00:05:25.992
I mean, I did --
we did our best.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:27.759
-I want to talk to you more

00:05:27.759 --> 00:05:30.129
about the New York Restoration
Project and what they do.

00:05:30.129 --> 00:05:32.732
We'll be right back with more
from Bette Midler.

